T = {
0: r'''\section{Introduction}
The structural response of a horizontal-axis wind turbine depends on the flexibility of the tower and blades, rotor motion, and the actions transmitted by the wind and foundation. Its description combines rotating components with components that remain nominally stationary. This coexistence requires both the configuration relative to which deformation is measured and the reference frame in which positions, velocities, and forces are expressed to be specified.''',
1: r'''The work of \citet{maza2014} and \citet{turello2023} provides background on wind turbine aeroelasticity and soil--structure interaction. Continuum mechanics distinguishes motion from deformation \citep{holzapfel2000}, while multibody dynamics organizes the relationships between rigid and flexible components \citep{shabana2013}. Finite-rotation beam formulations \citep{simo1985} provide a kinematic reference; the present model specializes to small strains and small structural rotations relative to a nominal rotation that may be arbitrarily large.''',
2: r'''The objective is to establish a structural formulation in which kinematics, physical properties, and equations of motion share a common convention. The exposition begins with the bodies and their reference frames, continues with sectional properties and discretization, and then derives the contributions of prescribed rotation. To this end, the decomposition of kinetic energy by degree in generalized velocities is distinguished from its subsequent expansion about the operating state \citep{meirovitch1997}. Kinematic constraints, external loads, and vibration and transient-response problems complete the development.''',
3: r'''\section{System description and kinematics}
\label{sec:cinematica}
A three-bladed wind turbine aligned with the mean wind and having a nominally horizontal axis is considered. The tower and blades are deformable; the nacelle and hub--nose assembly are treated as rigid bodies. The nacelle--hub connection allows small relative translations and rotations through equivalent stiffness and damping. The connections between the hub and blade roots are rigid. Figure~\ref{fig:modelo} identifies the components; base motion is transmitted through the tower.''',
5: r'''\subsection{Configurations and component convention}
A material body is the set of particles that constitutes a component. Its reference configuration $\mathcal B_0$ assigns a position $\bm X$ to each particle; its current configuration $\mathcal B_t$ contains the positions occupied at time $t$. Motion is expressed as''',
7: r'''The reference is the assembly geometry without elastic deformation of the components. The field $\bm U$ contains both rigid motion and deformation: rotor rotation can produce a large displacement without deforming the blades. The symbol $\bm u$ is therefore reserved for structural displacement relative to nominal motion, rather than total displacement $\bm U$.''',
8: r'''Vectors and tensors are written in bold; in matrix products, their components in the indicated basis are understood. The symbol $\tr$ denotes transposition, $\I$ is the three-dimensional identity, and $\bm a\otimes\bm b$ is the tensor product, whose matrix is $\bm a\bm b^\tr$. The skew-symmetric matrix associated with the cross product is defined by''',
10: r'''A rotation matrix transforms components without changing the physical vector. The convention is''',
12: r'''where the columns of $\bm Q_{AB}$ are the axes of basis $B$ expressed in $A$. These matrices satisfy $\bm Q^\tr\bm Q=\I$ and $\det\bm Q=1$. A second-order tensor transforms as $\bm J^A=\bm Q_{AB}\bm J^B\bm Q_{AB}^\tr$. Tensor and vector transformations therefore involve different operations.
Figure~\ref{fig:cambio-componentes} illustrates the distinction: the physical vector is unique, whereas its projections depend on the basis. The drawings use $\bm e_i^A$ for axis $i$ of frame $A$; its components in $N$ form column $i$ of $\bm Q_{NA}$.''',
14: r'''\subsection{Reference frames and nominal rotation}
The Newtonian frame $\mathcal F_N=\{O_N;\bm E_1,\bm E_2,\bm E_3\}$ is treated as inertial. The vector $\bm E_3$ is vertical, $\bm E_1$ points along the mean wind, and $\bm E_2=\bm E_3\times\bm E_1$. The base frame $\mathcal F_B$ has axes parallel to those of $N$ and origin $\bm c_B(t)=\bm c_{B0}+\bm u_g(t)$; $\bm u_g$ is the prescribed ground translation. Thus, $\bm Q_{NB}=\I$, but $B$ is not inertial when $\ddot{\bm u}_g\ne\bm0$.''',
15: r'''Frame $\mathcal F_G$ originates at the nacelle reference point. Its nominal orientation coincides with that of $N$. Frame $\mathcal F_H$ originates on the hub axis and rotates nominally relative to $G$. An overbar distinguishes nominal from perturbed orientations. Thus,''',
17: r'''The azimuth $\psi$ follows the right-hand rule about $\bm E_1$; $\psi_0$ is its initial value, and $\Omega$ is measured in radians per second. Nominal nacelle yaw to follow changes in wind direction is excluded. Small nacelle, hub, and tower vibrations are included in the structural response.
Figure~\ref{fig:sistemas-globales} locates the origins and the three directions of each frame in space. Figure~\ref{fig:giro-nominal} shows the rotation of the hub axes relative to the inertial axes. The superscript $H,n$ in the drawings denotes the nominal orientation represented by an overbar in the text.''',
19: r'''Each blade $b=1,2,3$ has a root frame $\mathcal F_{P_b}$. Axis 1 is longitudinal, axis 2 is tangential to the rotor, and axis 3 is normal to the rotor plane. For equally spaced blades without initial cone or pitch, define''',
21: r'''The first column of $\bm Q_{HP_b}$ is the radial direction $\bm d_{1b}^H=[0,-\sin\beta_b,\cos\beta_b]^\tr$. The second is $\bm d_{2b}^H=[0,-\cos\beta_b,-\sin\beta_b]^\tr$, and the third is $\bm d_{3b}^H=[1,0,0]^\tr$. Blade 1 points upward when $\psi=0$. If $r_h$ is the distance from the hub origin to the root, its nominal position is''',
23: r'''A fixed pitch angle $\alpha_b$ is introduced by right-multiplying $\bm Q_{HP_b}$ by $\bm R_1(\alpha_b)$, since it is a rotation about the blade longitudinal axis. This assembly orientation is distinct from azimuth and elastic twist.
Figure~\ref{fig:marcos} specializes the three root bases to zero azimuth. The first index of $\bm d_{ib}$ identifies the axis and the second identifies the blade.''',
25: r'''The rotation in Eq.~\eqref{eq:rotacion-nominal} is prescribed: it is supplied to the model rather than calculated from a drive balance. Nominal rotation is prescribed while small relative connection twist remains an unknown. Prescribing the exact instantaneous hub speed would also require constraining that coordinate. The tower and nacelle do not rotate nominally with the rotor; their displacements arise from base excitation and structural deformation.''',
26: r'''\subsection{Continuum kinematics of the components}
The position of a rigid-body particle is written as''',
28: r'''where $\bm c$ is the position of its reference point and $\bm\rho$ is the constant material vector from that point. Matrix $\bm Q$ transforms components from the body-fixed basis to the inertial basis. For a deformable beam, the cross-section origin and orientation also depend on the longitudinal material coordinate $S$:''',
30: r'''Here, $\bm R$ and $\bm r$ are the reference and current lines; $\xi_2,\xi_3$ locate the same particle within the section in both configurations. The directors $\bm D_i$ and $\bm d_i$ form orthonormal reference and current bases, respectively. Cross-sections remain planar and rigid in their own plane, but their normal $\bm d_1=\bm d_2\times\bm d_3$ need not coincide with the tangent to $\bm r$. This independence represents shear deformation in Timoshenko theory. No additional warping field is resolved; its torsional effect is included through an equivalent stiffness.''',
31: r'''Figure~\ref{fig:viga} locates a beam particle using the reference line and the section directors. Figure~\ref{fig:cortante} distinguishes the section normal from the tangent, making their kinematic independence explicit.''',
33: r'''For a straight blade, the nominal line position is obtained by adding $\overline{\bm Q}_{NP_b}[S,0,0]^\tr$ to Eq.~\eqref{eq:raices}. In the tower, $S$ runs from the base to the nacelle, with nominal triad $(\bm E_3,\bm E_1,\bm E_2)$. This defines the relationship between each beam's material coordinates and the machine geometry.''',
34: r'''Perturbation rotation is represented by $\bm E(\bm\theta)=\exp(\Sx{\theta})$. For the nacelle and hub, $\bm Q=\overline{\bm Q}\bm E$; the same relation applies to a section after including its assembly orientation. Vector $\bm\theta$ expresses the angular increment in the corresponding nominal basis. It is generally neither a vector of Euler angles nor a quantity whose derivative equals angular velocity exactly. That relationship is specified when the inertial terms are derived.''',
35: r'''Figure~\ref{fig:configuraciones} compares the material reference, nominal motion, and perturbed motion. The position a particle would have under nominal motion is denoted by $\overline{\bm x}(\bm X,t)$, and the corresponding configuration by $\overline{\mathcal B}_t$. The difference $\bm x-\overline{\bm x}$ is its displacement relative to that trajectory; in a beam, this includes both translation of the section origin and the change in section orientation.''',
37: r'''Deformation is measured relative to the material geometry, independently of the observer. For example, if $\bm Q_s=[\bm d_1,\bm d_2,\bm d_3]$ and $\bm Q_{s0}=[\bm D_1,\bm D_2,\bm D_3]$, the extension--shear and curvature--twist measures are''',
39: r'''A prime denotes differentiation with respect to $S$, and $\operatorname{axial}([\bm a]_\times)=\bm a$. A superposed rigid motion leaves these measures unchanged. For an initially straight beam, their linear approximation in structural translations and rotations is used; nominal rotation is retained separately.''',
40: r'''\subsection{Operating state and perturbations}
The material geometry $\mathcal B_0$, prescribed nominal trajectory, and structural equilibrium configuration $\bm q_0$ are distinguished. The latter depends on loads, rotational speed, and component properties. Structural coordinates are written as $\bm q(t)=\bm q_0+\delta\bm q(t)$; $\delta\bm q$ describes vibrations without including the prescribed azimuth again.''',
41: r'''When loads and coefficients are stationary in the chosen coordinates, $\bm q_0$ defines relative equilibrium: deformation is constant in those coordinates although rotor particles continue moving. Gravity fixed in the inertial frame or nonuniform flow may prevent this stationarity. In that case, equilibrium at a given azimuth and its linearization are a local approximation, rather than a complete periodic solution. Reference mass properties are retained; preload modifies the tangent stiffness within the small-deformation assumption.''',
42: r'''\FloatBarrier
\section{Section properties and mass}
\subsection{Geometric and mass moments of inertia}
Properties are defined on a material section $A_0(S)$ using reference density $\rho_0$ and local coordinates $y,z$. The geometric second-moment tensor about the geometric center is''',
44: r'''This gives $I_y=\int_{A_0}z^2\dd A$, $I_z=\int_{A_0}y^2\dd A$, and the polar moment $I_y+I_z$. The area is $A=\int_{A_0}\dd A$. In principal axes and for a section without constitutive coupling, the sectional stiffness matrix is''',
46: r'''where $E$ is Young's modulus, $G$ is the shear modulus, $A_{sy},A_{sz}$ are effective shear areas, and $J_t$ is the torsional constant. The effective areas already include the correction for the shear-stress distribution. The quantities $I_y,I_z,J_t$ have units of $\mathrm{m^4}$, but $J_t$ does not generally equal $I_y+I_z$. The matrix relates generalized strains to their work-conjugate resultants:''',
48: r'''The components of $\bm n$ are the axial and two shear forces; those of $\bm m_s$ are the torsional and two bending moments. They follow by integrating sectional traction $\bm t$ and its moment: $\bm n=\int_{A_0}\bm t\,\dd A$ and $\bm m_s=\int_{A_0}\bm\xi\times\bm t\,\dd A$, within the small-deformation approximation. Their work per unit length is $\bm n\cdot\delta\bm\gamma+\bm m_s\cdot\delta\bm\kappa$. A composite or eccentric section may require a full symmetric constitutive matrix; the diagonal expression~\eqref{eq:Ks} assumes those couplings are negligible.''',
49: r'''Mass properties use the sectional center of mass $C$, which need not coincide with the geometric center. If $\bm\rho_C$ connects $C$ to a sectional particle,''',
51: r'''In centroidal principal axes, $j_y=\int_{A_0}\rho_0 z^2\dd A$, $j_z=\int_{A_0}\rho_0 y^2\dd A$, and $j_x=j_y+j_z$ for a planar section. The units of $\mu$ and $\bm j_C$ are $\mathrm{kg/m}$ and $\mathrm{kg\,m}$. Off-diagonal entries are the negatives of the integrated coordinate products. This distinguishes a mass-inertia tensor from a geometric second moment of area.''',
52: r'''For a homogeneous isotropic material, $G=E/[2(1+\nu)]$, where $\nu$ is Poisson's ratio; also, $\mu=\rho_0 A$ and $\bm j_C=\rho_0\operatorname{diag}(I_y+I_z,I_y,I_z)$. Blade stiffnesses and mass properties may come directly from a sectional analysis. If a tabulated distribution must be normalized to a known total mass $m_b^\star$ while preserving its spatial shape and radii of gyration, use''',
54: r'''Here, $L_b$ is blade length, and superscript $\mathrm{tab}$ identifies the input data. Factor $c_b$ is dimensionless and applies to the full tensor. This normalization does not determine eccentricities or inertia products absent from the data. Assuming them to be zero is an additional idealization that must be consistent with the adopted section axes.''',
55: r'''\subsection{Spatial mass matrix}
Let $\bm r_C$ be the vector from the section origin to its center of mass, expressed in the same basis as the velocities. The centroidal velocity is $\bm v_C=\bm v_O+\bm\omega_s\times\bm r_C$, where $\bm v_O$ and $\bm\omega_s$ are the origin velocity and section angular velocity. Integrating particle energy and using the centroidal condition eliminates the cross term about $C$, giving''',
57: r'''Figure~\ref{fig:masa-seccional} distinguishes the reference-line origin, center of mass, and particle position. Vector $\bm r_C+\bm\rho_C$ locates the particle from the origin; tensor $\bm j_C$ in Eq.~\eqref{eq:sectioninertia} instead uses distances measured from $C$.''',
59: r'''To express this energy relative to the section origin, introduce''',
61: r'''In a stationary reference and at first kinematic order, $[\bm v_O^\tr,\bm\omega_s^\tr]^\tr=\dot{\bm\eta}$, with $\bm\eta=[\bm u^\tr,\bm\theta^\tr]^\tr$. The quadratic energy is then''',
63: r'''The matrix multiplying these generalized velocities is therefore''',
65: r'''The block $-\mu\Sx{r_C}^2=\mu[(\bm r_C\cdot\bm r_C)\I-\bm r_C\otimes\bm r_C]$ follows from the parallel-axis theorem. Cross blocks represent translation--rotation coupling due to eccentricity. During nominal rotation, the energy contains additional terms, which are derived rather than identifying total angular velocity with $\dot{\bm\theta}$.''',
66: r'''For a rigid body, replace $\mu$ by $m=\int_{\mathcal B_0}\rho_0\,\dd V$ and $\bm j_C$ by $\bm J_C=\int_{\mathcal B_0}\rho_0[(\bm\rho_C\cdot\bm\rho_C)\I-\bm\rho_C\otimes\bm\rho_C]\,\dd V$, with units of $\mathrm{kg\,m^2}$. Transformation~\eqref{eq:cambio-base} expresses the tensor in the assembly basis, and the parallel-axis theorem refers it to the chosen nacelle or hub point. Orientation and eccentricity act on the full tensor, rather than only its diagonal entries.''',
67: r'''\section{Structural discretization}
\label{sec:elementos}
\subsection{Nodal variables and interpolation}
Each beam reference line is divided into two-node elements. Each node has three structural translations and three rotations, so $\bm q_i=[\bm u_i^\tr,\bm\theta_i^\tr]^\tr$ and each element has twelve coordinates before constraints. Local coordinate $x\in[0,L]$ spans the element reference length, corresponding to an interval of material coordinate $S$. Local axes $x,y,z$ coincide with section axes 1, 2, and 3. In these axes, linearizing Eq.~\eqref{eq:medidas-viga} for the field $\bm\eta=[u,v,w,\theta_x,\theta_y,\theta_z]^\tr$ gives''',
69: r'''The first three components are extension and shear; the last three are twist and curvatures. A prime now denotes differentiation with respect to $x$. Interpolation is $\bm\eta=\bm N(x)\bm q_e^\ell$, where $\bm N$ has size $6\times12$ and $\ell$ identifies local components. Substitution into the strain relations gives $\bm\varepsilon=\bm B(x)\bm q_e^\ell$. Internal virtual work and kinetic energy yield the element matrices''',
71: r'''A closed-form two-node interpolation is used for uncoupled properties constant within each element \citep{reddy2006}. Stiffness blocks and the functions used for mass are detailed in Appendix A. The limit $GA_s\to\infty$ removes shear deformation; if rotary inertia is retained, the limiting dynamic model is of Rayleigh type, rather than classical Euler--Bernoulli.''',
72: r'''\subsection{Orientation and assembly}
The reference end positions $\bm X_I,\bm X_J$ and an auxiliary point $\bm X_a$ determine the element basis:''',
74: r'''The auxiliary point must lie off the axis and is chosen to reproduce the sectional assembly directions. With $\bm Q_e=[\bm e_1,\bm e_2,\bm e_3]$ and $\bm R_e=\bm Q_e^\tr$,''',
76: r'''Superscript $g$ identifies assembly components, whose axes are parallel to those of $N$ for the geometry considered. The symbol $\operatorname{diag}$ with matrix arguments denotes a block-diagonal matrix. Conservation of virtual work gives $\bm K_e^g=\bm T_e^\tr\bm K_e^\ell\bm T_e$ and $\bm M_e^g=\bm T_e^\tr\bm M_e^\ell\bm T_e$. This transformation at a given configuration does not replace the time-dependent transformation of velocities and accelerations.''',
77: r'''Figure~\ref{fig:elemento} identifies the element ends and the projected auxiliary direction used to construct its spatial basis. The auxiliary point fixes only the transverse axes; it is not a node with degrees of freedom.''',
79: r'''If $\bm L_e$ extracts the element coordinates from $\bm q$, a generic matrix $\bm A$, such as mass or stiffness, is assembled as''',
81: r'''where $\bm L_R$ locates rigid-body and connection contributions in global coordinates. Kinematic constraints are imposed after assembly, so coordinates on both sides of each connection are still retained.''',
82: r'''\subsection{Nacelle--hub connection}
The nacelle and hub reference points $G,H$ are nominally separated by $\bm d=\overline{\bm c}_H-\overline{\bm c}_G$. In a common basis, the relative connection deformation is''',
84: r'''Matrix $\bm B_d$ collects the coefficients of this relation; its columns corresponding to $[\bm u_G,\bm\theta_G,\bm u_H,\bm\theta_H]$ are $[-\I,\Sx{d},\I,\bm0]$ in the translational block and $[\bm0,-\I,\bm0,\I]$ in the angular block. Constitutive matrices $\bm k_d,\bm c_d$ relate forces and moments to $\bm\Delta_d,\dot{\bm\Delta}_d$. This gives $\bm K_d=\bm B_d^\tr\bm k_d\bm B_d$ and $\bm C_d=\bm B_d^\tr\bm c_d\bm B_d$. The arm removes spurious deformation under common rigid motion. Relative drivetrain twist is an elastic unknown distinct from nominal rotation; with positive torsional stiffness, it is not a zero-frequency mechanism.''',
}
